WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.419 --> 00:00:09.840
So, I would like to start by telling 
you the story of a woman

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.651
who died in Nevada in last August.

00:00:12.651 --> 00:00:14.868
Her death has brought the discussion

00:00:14.868 --> 00:00:17.441
of antibiotics resistance 
to the news once again.

00:00:17.441 --> 00:00:20.580
Basically, she traveled to India 
and while she was there,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.200
she broke her right leg.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.041
And then when she returned to the US,

00:00:24.041 --> 00:00:26.371
she went to the hospital 
to treat her leg injury

00:00:26.371 --> 00:00:28.160
and while she was doing some exams,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.950
the doctors discovered she was with 
an infection in her leg and hip bones.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.841
But the doctors also determined that 
no amount of antibiotics

00:00:37.841 --> 00:00:40.262
would be enough to save her life

00:00:40.262 --> 00:00:43.560
because she was infected 
with multiresistant bacteria.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.749
They were immune to all 
the drugs they had.

00:00:46.749 --> 00:00:49.418
So there was nothing 
they could do to help her.

00:00:49.418 --> 00:00:53.160
I would love to tell you 
this is an unusual case or an exception,

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:58.190
that antibiotics can treat
the bacterial infections we have today,

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:01.689
but actually, that's no longer true.

00:01:01.689 --> 00:01:05.401
Do you have any idea of how many 
people die each year

00:01:05.401 --> 00:01:08.399
due to this kind of infection?

00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:11.069
700,000 more or less.

00:01:11.069 --> 00:01:17.041
That might not seem so much when compared
to the 6.4 million deaths caused by stroke

00:01:17.041 --> 00:01:22.429
or even the 8.76 million deaths 
caused by heart disease.

00:01:22.429 --> 00:01:26.099
However, the predictions 
show that by 2050,

00:01:26.099 --> 00:01:31.180
resistant bacteria will kill around 
10 million people a year

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.834
if nothing is done to stop them,

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:37.560
which means that stories like the one 
I told you will become more common

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:44.370
than stories of people dying
of stroke, heart disease, or cancer.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:49.543
If we think that around 60 million 
people died in World War II

00:01:49.543 --> 00:01:52.341
during the six years the war lasted,

00:01:52.341 --> 00:01:58.130
it will be the same number of deaths 
per year as World War II.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:05.938
That means the same number of casualties
of our history's deadliest conflict.

00:02:05.938 --> 00:02:10.150
We thought antibiotics would forever 
protect us from bacteria, right?

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:14.796
But the current situation 
is showing us that's wrong.

00:02:14.796 --> 00:02:18.434
So as I told you, 
we are facing a war against bacteria,

00:02:18.434 --> 00:02:21.230
but who are the characters in this war?

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:26.632
So we can compare the harmful bacteria 
to an enemy army,

00:02:26.632 --> 00:02:31.131
our immune cells 
to the soldiers of our army,

00:02:31.131 --> 00:02:35.879
and antibiotics to the weapons of our army
in this crazy battle.

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:37.650
If you are in an actual war,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.169
do you think it is clever to keep 
constantly attacking your enemy

00:02:42.169 --> 00:02:47.520
using the same weapons and strategies?

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.129
No, right?

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:53.030
Of course not, because you make it
easier for your opponents to defeat you,

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.114
They can analyze your attack methods,

00:02:55.114 --> 00:02:57.049
build up defenses,

00:02:57.049 --> 00:03:01.590
and then attack you back
with the advantages on their side.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.220
And now you might be thinking,

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:08.451
"But that's quite predictable, isn't it?
We can't have been so naive to do that."

00:03:08.451 --> 00:03:12.174
And the answer is, yes, we were.

00:03:12.174 --> 00:03:15.893
Bacteria have been doing this analysis 
for more than 70 years,

00:03:15.893 --> 00:03:19.636
eventually discovering 
the resistance genes,

00:03:19.636 --> 00:03:23.472
which is a way they found to protect 
themselves from our antibiotics,

00:03:23.472 --> 00:03:25.325
and this is how this happens.

00:03:25.325 --> 00:03:28.816
Imagine you have a person
that is with an infection in their guts.

00:03:28.816 --> 00:03:34.134
And there's the bacteria colony
that's making this infection.

00:03:34.134 --> 00:03:39.234
And in this colony, one bacterium
is resistant to antibiotics' effects.

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:41.453
Then the person takes the antibiotics,

00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:43.884
and as they get in contact 
with the colony,

00:03:43.884 --> 00:03:48.346
the regular bacteria starts to die,
but the resistant one doesn't.

00:03:48.346 --> 00:03:54.324
And now it finds itself with the whole
space and all the nutrients for itself

00:03:54.324 --> 00:03:56.821
and then it starts to grow 
and grow and grow,

00:03:56.821 --> 00:04:02.411
and in the end, we have another colony
of now-resistant bacteria.

00:04:02.411 --> 00:04:07.682
So as you can see, antibiotics sometimes
don't solve our problem

00:04:07.682 --> 00:04:09.560
because they don't kill the bacteria.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.690
They instead select the resistant ones,

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:17.334
and this is how antibiotics excessive use
becomes a problem

00:04:17.334 --> 00:04:19.324
because the more we use them,

00:04:19.324 --> 00:04:24.732
the more we increase the chances of ending
up selecting resistant bacteria.

00:04:24.732 --> 00:04:26.884
And to make matters worse,

00:04:26.884 --> 00:04:29.804
resistant bacteria can share
the resistant genes,

00:04:29.804 --> 00:04:31.832
which is that circular DNA there,

00:04:31.832 --> 00:04:35.151
with other bacteria that were
previously not resistant,

00:04:35.151 --> 00:04:37.799
allowing not only new bacteria
to become resistant

00:04:37.799 --> 00:04:41.454
but also to become resistant
to other types of drugs,

00:04:41.454 --> 00:04:46.572
because one resistant gene is
effective against one kind of antibiotic.

00:04:46.572 --> 00:04:48.883
And they do this process 
in a very simple way,

00:04:48.883 --> 00:04:51.091
much like a friend that comes
close to you,

00:04:51.091 --> 00:04:55.892
you give him or her a present, a gift,
and then they leave.

00:04:55.892 --> 00:04:57.012
That's it.

00:04:57.012 --> 00:04:59.912
So, there you have one bacterium
that has the resistant gene

00:04:59.912 --> 00:05:01.203
and another that doesn't.

00:05:01.203 --> 00:05:02.882
So they get very close together,

00:05:02.882 --> 00:05:04.493
they share their inner parts.

00:05:04.493 --> 00:05:07.074
The resistant bacteria makes a copy
of that gene,

00:05:07.074 --> 00:05:08.331
gives it to the other,

00:05:08.331 --> 00:05:11.442
and then they split 
and now both are resistant.

00:05:11.442 --> 00:05:16.095
And since bacteria have been doing
this process since 1945

00:05:16.095 --> 00:05:19.294
when the first resistant bacteria
were found,

00:05:19.294 --> 00:05:24.022
today there are certain kinds of bacteria
that are immune to almost all,

00:05:24.022 --> 00:05:27.052
or even all, antibiotics we have

00:05:27.052 --> 00:05:29.531
because they have found 
all the ingredients

00:05:29.531 --> 00:05:32.833
for this global resistance recipe,

00:05:32.833 --> 00:05:36.572
which means there's no cure for them.

00:05:36.572 --> 00:05:39.153
We use antibiotics to make animals 
get fat faster

00:05:39.153 --> 00:05:41.463
so we can slay them and sell their meat,

00:05:41.463 --> 00:05:43.813
to protect plants 
from agricultural plagues,

00:05:43.813 --> 00:05:46.832
people self-medicate in many, 
many countries

00:05:46.832 --> 00:05:49.089
and even doctors sometimes make mistakes

00:05:49.089 --> 00:05:53.082
and prescribe antibiotics 
for diseases they can't treat.

00:05:53.082 --> 00:05:57.894
We can't do these things anymore in the
pace we have been doing in the last years

00:05:57.894 --> 00:06:01.573
if we still want antibiotics protection.

00:06:01.573 --> 00:06:05.742
Alexander Fleming received his Nobel Award
for discovering penicillin,

00:06:05.742 --> 00:06:07.602
the first antibiotic.

00:06:07.602 --> 00:06:11.657
He warned us about the consequences 
of using antibiotics excessively,

00:06:11.657 --> 00:06:16.044
advice that was ignored 
by the pharmaceutical industry.

00:06:16.044 --> 00:06:17.231
According to him,

00:06:17.231 --> 00:06:20.454
"The thoughtless person playing 
with penicillin treatment

00:06:20.454 --> 00:06:23.423
is morally responsible 
for the death of a man

00:06:23.423 --> 00:06:26.812
who succumbs
to a penicillin-resistant organism."

00:06:26.812 --> 00:06:28.772
There is another problem,

00:06:28.772 --> 00:06:31.894
which is the consequences 
of these drugs in our bodies.

00:06:31.894 --> 00:06:35.901
So, antibiotics are drugs 
that are made to kill bacteria,

00:06:35.901 --> 00:06:40.742
making no distinction between the bacteria
that are harmful or helpful to us.

00:06:40.742 --> 00:06:44.701
Our bodies are a great 
environment for bacteria.

00:06:44.701 --> 00:06:50.357
Believe it or not, your body has ten times
more bacteria cells than human cells.

00:06:50.357 --> 00:06:55.210
There are certain bacteria that live 
in our guts that are so important to us

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.851
that we could not live without them.

00:06:57.851 --> 00:07:02.143
They could be compared to the people
in this microscopic war

00:07:02.143 --> 00:07:05.982
helping us, the nation,
and our immune cells, the soldiers,

00:07:05.982 --> 00:07:07.492
to face this war.

00:07:07.492 --> 00:07:10.902
They help us in digestion
to absorb essential nutrients

00:07:10.902 --> 00:07:13.653
they fight harmful microorganisms,

00:07:13.653 --> 00:07:16.491
and they also help us recover from illness

00:07:16.491 --> 00:07:22.312
because they stimulate our immune system
to produce immune cells, or more soldiers.

00:07:22.312 --> 00:07:27.564
When we take antibiotics, we are removing
part of these natural defenses

00:07:27.564 --> 00:07:30.492
which makes us feel even weaker,

00:07:30.492 --> 00:07:33.102
and although there are ways to make up

00:07:33.102 --> 00:07:36.283
for the damage caused 
by antibiotics in our gut flora,

00:07:36.283 --> 00:07:44.246
few people take the time to make
the proper supplementation of probiotics,

00:07:44.246 --> 00:07:46.355
or these helpful bacteria,

00:07:46.355 --> 00:07:50.425
by eating cheese, yogurt,
or probiotic capsules.

00:07:50.425 --> 00:07:56.155
Therefore we need to find new weapons and
strategies to turn the tide in this war.

00:07:56.155 --> 00:08:00.381
And so the possible solutions are 
the development of new drugs,

00:08:00.381 --> 00:08:04.524
not only new antibiotics,
new and different antibiotics,

00:08:04.524 --> 00:08:06.630
but new vaccines as well,

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:09.071
improving sanitation conditions,

00:08:09.071 --> 00:08:13.760
cutting off the use of antibiotics 
in livestock and agriculture,

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.030
and start using antibiotics 
wisely and consciously,

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:23.970
aware of the consequences 
that it can cause to us.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.699
And the last one, 
and most important of all,

00:08:26.699 --> 00:08:30.059
make other people aware, as well.

00:08:30.059 --> 00:08:34.111
As we can't just go to a lab and 
create a new drug to solve the problem,

00:08:34.111 --> 00:08:37.200
we must help the way we can:

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.032
washing our hands,

00:08:39.032 --> 00:08:41.919
wearing warm clothes during the winter,

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:43.550
eating fruits and vegetables,

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:47.001
basically everything our mothers 
have told us to do so many times

00:08:47.001 --> 00:08:48.770
in order to prevent illness,

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.469
it's very important, too.

00:08:50.469 --> 00:08:53.540
Share this information.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:54.959
Make people aware.

00:08:54.959 --> 00:08:58.881
Explain why we should stop
using antibiotics excessively.

00:08:58.881 --> 00:09:04.491
Antibiotics are such a valuable tool
and we rely on them for so many things.

00:09:04.491 --> 00:09:08.346
We can't just lose them because 
of our irresponsibility.

00:09:08.346 --> 00:09:11.286
The future is in our hands now.

00:09:11.286 --> 00:09:16.844
We can either hold it, 
or let it fall between our fingers

00:09:16.844 --> 00:09:18.625
Let's make the right choice.

00:09:18.625 --> 00:09:19.845
Thank you.

